diff --git a/ARCHITECTURE.md b/ARCHITECTURE.md
index a4eee0a..649b9bd 100644
--- a/ARCHITECTURE.md
+++ b/ARCHITECTURE.md
@@ -6,9 +6,9 @@ The model labels and explains; Python counts. Features are computed in Python, t
 
 ![Architecture diagram](docs/architecture.png)
 
-Green boxes are built and tested; dashed orange boxes are planned. The editable source is `docs/architecture.mmd` (Mermaid); re-draw it with `mmdc -i docs/architecture.mmd -o docs/architecture.png -w 1800 -s 2 -b white`.
+Every box is built and tested. The editable source is `docs/architecture.mmd` (Mermaid); re-draw it with `mmdc -i docs/architecture.mmd -o docs/architecture.png -s 2 -b white -p docs/mmdc-config.json -c docs/mermaid-config.json` (older mermaid-cli versions take `-w 1800` in place of the mermaid config).
 
-Training runs on Colab; everything else runs on the laptop.
+Training runs on Colab. Everything else runs on the laptop, including the live run: an uploaded alert takes the same features, wording, prompt and output checks as the training data, so a live answer is produced and checked exactly like a saved one. The model itself runs either on the laptop (`local_model.py`) or on a hosted GPU (`remote_model.py` calling `model_server.py` on Modal); when neither is reachable the alert goes to "needs review" and the rules and computed findings still run.
 
 ## Modules
 
@@ -34,7 +34,13 @@ Training runs on Colab; everything else runs on the laptop.
 | `src/case_store.py` | Case memory: one JSON case file per alert, added to by each step |
 | `src/audit.py` | The audit log: append-only JSON lines, one per step or decision |
 | `src/review.py`, `src/review_scores.py` | Join alerts, rules and saved model answers for the app; the Evaluation tab numbers |
-| `app.py` | Streamlit UI: Alerts, Alert detail with the investigation, Evaluation, Audit log |
+| `src/upload.py` | Reads an uploaded transactions CSV into a `RawAlert`; refuses a bad file with a message naming the row |
+| `src/live.py` | The live run: uploaded alert → features → summary → model → checks, joined into the same `ReviewAlert` the saved alerts use |
+| `src/local_model.py` | Loads the base model plus adapter on this computer (`AML_LOCAL_MODEL=1`); nothing leaves the machine |
+| `src/remote_model.py` | Calls the hosted model over HTTPS; address and Modal proxy auth token come from the environment |
+| `model_server.py` | Hosts the model on a Modal GPU that sleeps when idle; uses `src/predict.py` to load it |
+| `app.py` | Streamlit UI: Alerts, Alert detail with the investigation, Live run, Evaluation, Audit log |
+| `upload_view.py` | The Live run tab: upload form, profile fields, and the result drawn like any other alert |
 | `notebooks/finetune_qlora.ipynb` | Thin Colab wrapper that clones the repo and calls `src/finetune.py` |
 
 ## Data model
@@ -108,10 +114,14 @@ Metrics: per-typology precision, recall and F1; macro-F1; false-positive rate on
 | Larger base model | `finetune.py` model argument; keep the same prompt format |
 | LLM planner | Replace the fixed sequence in `investigator.py`, keeping the same guardrails |
 | Persistent audit log | Replace session state in `app.py` with a store keyed by case id |
+| Another model host | Add a `generate` function beside `local_model.py` and `remote_model.py`; the rest of the live path is unchanged |
 
 ## Known limits
 
 - All data is synthetic, so scores say nothing about real-world accuracy.
 - Thresholds are illustrative, not regulatory.
 - Corrections live in session state and are lost when the session ends.
-- CPU inference of the fine-tuned model is slow; the demo can use precomputed verdicts.
+- The shared demo on Streamlit Community Cloud has no GPU, so its sample alerts use saved verdicts and uploads there go to "needs review".
+- On a laptop the model answers one alert in roughly 15 to 20 seconds, and the first load takes a few minutes.
+- The published scores came from the 4-bit model on a T4; `local_model.py` loads it at half precision instead, which was not the setup scored.
+- An uploaded file must already use the eight channel names the generator uses; real bank exports would need a mapping step.
